  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The motion of a mass on a spring, with spring constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> is as shown in figure.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0092_1671511e07c8.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 197px;\" /></p><p>The equation of motion is given by,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#969;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></math>&nbsp;with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#969;</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math>.<br />Suppose that at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo></math>the position of mass is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math> and velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;then its displacement can also be represented as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#981;</mi></math> are&nbsp;</p>"
